    "result_text": "The only test class in the suite is `AcceptanceTest` (the other two files are support/base classes), and it's green. Full build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change emits an immutable structured `OWNER_CREATED` event to the `AUDIT` logger, alongside the existing audit line.\n\n**What I did, kept to low structural impact:**\n\n- **New cohesive unit** \u2014 `service/OwnerCreatedEvent.java`. It owns the `AtomicInteger` `seq` (monotonically increasing across all creates) and emits the JSON `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`. The event's value for the primary identifier is read through a tiny private `primaryId(Owner)` method \u2014 so when `customerCode` is later unified into `memberId`, only that one line changes and the event automatically carries the `memberId`. This class has near-zero surrounding complexity (WMC_other \u2248 0), so it's cheap to write and to evolve.\n\n- **One-line touch to the hot method** \u2014 in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` I added a single `OwnerCreatedEvent.emit(owner);` call plus one import. `addOwner` lives in a large, high-`WMC_other` controller, so I deliberately minimized `changed_lines` there (1 line inside the method) and put all new logic in the small class instead of growing the method or the class.\n\nTwo files touched; each changed function stays simple.\n\nVerification: `AcceptanceTest` passes and the emitted event line is, e.g.:\n`{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-00C7EBD7\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
